replies.text:
I'll analyze EVAL-05 for security threats using STRIDE methodology and produce a threat model.

Based on the context, this is a SAML single sign-on implementation for an internal admin portal that's internet-accessible. The portal reads role attributes from SAML assertions to grant admin access. This creates several security risks around authentication, authorization, and XML processing.

Let me build the threat model:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-05",
  "sketch": {
    "components": [
      "Admin portal web application",
      "SAML identity provider",
      "SAML assertion processor",
      "Role-based access control",
      "Session management",
      "Authentication endpoint"
    ],
    "dataStores": [
      "User sessions",
      "Audit logs"
    ],
    "externalServices": [
      "Corporate identity provider"
    ],
    "trustBoundaries": [
      "Internet to admin portal",
      "IdP to portal (SAML assertion)",
      "Portal to admin functions"
    ],
    "dataClasses": [
      "credentials"
    ],
    "exposure": "public",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Unsigned SAML assertion accepted as valid",
      "scenario": "An attacker crafts a SAML assertion claiming admin role without a valid signature. If the portal doesn't validate signatures, the attacker gains admin access by sending the forged assertion.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the portal reads the role attribute from the assertion to decide admin access"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-347"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could create a fake login token claiming to be an admin, and if the system doesn't check if it's real, they'd get full access.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify SAML assertion signatures",
        "given": "a SAML assertion is received from the identity provider",
        "when": "processing authentication",
        "then": "the portal must cryptographically verify the assertion's XML signature using the IdP's public certificate before trusting any attributes"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "XML Signature Wrapping attack on SAML response",
      "scenario": "An attacker intercepts a valid SAML response, wraps the signed assertion in a modified envelope with attacker-controlled role claims, and exploits loose XML parsing to have the signature validate while the portal reads the malicious role attribute.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-347"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could take a real login response, hide fake admin permissions inside it, and trick the system into accepting both the real signature and the fake permissions.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent XML signature wrapping",
        "given": "the portal parses SAML XML responses",
        "when": "validating signatures",
        "then": "the parser must validate that signed elements match referenced elements and reject responses with duplicate or wrapped signed content"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Role attribute injection via SAML assertion manipulation",
      "scenario": "An attacker with valid credentials modifies their own SAML assertion in transit to change the role attribute from 'user' to 'admin', gaining unauthorized administrative access because the role is read directly without additional validation.",
      "component": "Role-based access control",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the portal reads the role attribute from the assertion to decide admin access"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.1", "V4.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-269"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could change their role from 'regular user' to 'admin' in the login data, and the system would trust it without double-checking.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate role attributes within signed assertion",
        "given": "the portal extracts role information from SAML assertions",
        "when": "authorizing admin access",
        "then": "role attributes must be within the signed portion of the assertion and validated against an allowlist of expected values"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "IdP certificate validation bypass allows impersonation",
      "scenario": "An attacker sets up a malicious identity provider with a self-signed or expired certificate. If the portal doesn't properly validate the IdP certificate, the attacker can issue valid-looking SAML assertions and authenticate as any user including admins.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on with the corporate identity provider"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V9.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-295"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could pretend to be your company's login system, and if the app doesn't check the security certificate properly, it would accept fake logins.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Validate IdP certificates properly",
        "given": "the portal receives SAML assertions",
        "when": "verifying assertion signatures",
        "then": "the IdP certificate must be validated for expiry, trust chain, and hostname, and pinned to the expected corporate IdP certificate"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "XXE attack via malicious SAML XML",
      "scenario": "An attacker who can influence SAML responses injects XML external entity references that, when parsed by the portal, read sensitive files from the server (/etc/passwd, application config with secrets) or perform SSRF attacks against internal services.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.5.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-611"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could sneak commands into the login data that make the server read its own secret files and send them back to the attacker.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Disable XML external entities in SAML parsing",
        "given": "the portal parses SAML XML responses",
        "when": "processing authentication",
        "then": "the XML parser must have external entity resolution disabled and DTD processing disabled"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "XML bomb causes resource exhaustion",
      "scenario": "An attacker sends a SAML response containing a billion laughs XML bomb (nested entity expansion). When parsed, it consumes excessive CPU and memory, crashing the portal or making it unresponsive for legitimate admin users.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The portal is reachable from the internet"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.5.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-776"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could send specially crafted login data that makes the server use all its memory trying to process it, causing the whole admin system to crash.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Limit XML entity expansion",
        "given": "the portal parses SAML XML",
        "when": "processing authentication requests",
        "then": "the parser must limit entity expansion depth, entity expansion count, and total expanded size"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Session fixation after SAML authentication",
      "scenario": "An attacker tricks a victim into using a session ID the attacker knows. After the victim authenticates via SAML, the portal doesn't regenerate the session ID, so the attacker can use their known session ID to hijack the victim's authenticated admin session.",
      "component": "Session management",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-384"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could give you a session cookie, and after you log in, they could use that same cookie to access your admin account.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Regenerate session tokens after authentication",
        "given": "a user successfully authenticates via SAML",
        "when": "creating the authenticated session",
        "then": "the portal must generate a new session identifier and invalidate any pre-authentication session ID"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Insufficient audit logging of admin actions",
      "scenario": "A malicious admin performs unauthorized actions (deleting data, changing configurations). Without comprehensive audit logs linking actions to SAML authentication events, the organization cannot prove who performed the actions or hold them accountable.",
      "component": "Admin portal web application",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the portal reads the role attribute from the assertion to decide admin access"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If admins do something wrong or malicious, there's no reliable record of who did what and when, so you can't prove what happened.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log authentication and admin actions with SAML identity",
        "given": "a user authenticates via SAML or performs admin actions",
        "when": "these events occur",
        "then": "the portal must log the timestamp, SAML NameID, asserted role, source IP, and action details in a tamper-evident audit log"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "SAML response replay attack",
      "scenario": "An attacker intercepts a valid SAML response and replays it later to authenticate without knowledge of credentials. If the portal doesn't validate NotOnOrAfter timestamps or track used assertion IDs, the replay succeeds.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-294"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could record your login once and then reuse that same recorded login multiple times to get in, like photocopying a ticket.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent SAML assertion replay",
        "given": "a SAML assertion is received",
        "when": "validating it for authentication",
        "then": "the portal must check NotOnOrAfter/NotBefore conditions, track used assertion IDs in a time-limited cache, and reject duplicates"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Detailed error messages expose system implementation",
      "scenario": "When SAML authentication fails, the portal returns verbose error messages revealing XML parsing details, library versions, or internal file paths. An attacker uses this information to identify vulnerabilities and craft targeted attacks.",
      "component": "Authentication endpoint",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 2,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The portal is reachable from the internet"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.4.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-209"],
      "plainLanguage": "When login fails, the error messages tell attackers exactly what went wrong and how the system works, helping them figure out how to break in.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Return generic authentication error messages",
        "given": "SAML authentication fails",
        "when": "returning errors to the client",
        "then": "the portal must return a generic 'authentication failed' message without exposing parsing details, library versions, or internal paths"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "No rate limiting on authentication endpoint",
      "scenario": "An attacker sends thousands of authentication requests to the portal from distributed IPs. Without rate limiting, the portal's resources are exhausted processing SAML responses, making it unavailable for legitimate admin users.",
      "component": "Authentication endpoint",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The portal is reachable from the internet"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-307"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could flood the login page with thousands of attempts, overwhelming the server and preventing real employees from logging in.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Rate limit authentication requests",
        "given": "the authentication endpoint receives requests",
        "when": "under normal or attack conditions",
        "then": "the portal must implement rate limiting per IP and per user to prevent brute force and DoS attacks"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Missing TLS on SAML communication",
      "scenario": "If SAML assertions are transmitted over unencrypted HTTP, an attacker on the network path captures the assertion containing role information and potentially session tokens, then uses them to impersonate the user.",
      "component": "Authentication endpoint",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The portal is reachable from the internet"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V9.1.1", "V9.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-319"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the connection isn't encrypted, someone on the network could read all the login data as it passes by, including who you are and what access you have.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce TLS for all SAML communication",
        "given": "SAML assertions are exchanged",
        "when": "communication occurs between IdP and portal",
        "then": "all SAML endpoints must require TLS 1.2+ with strong cipher suites and reject plaintext HTTP requests"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Single logout not implemented",
      "scenario": "A user authenticates to the admin portal via SAML but there's no logout mechanism. When they close their browser, their session may remain active. If someone else uses their computer, that person gains admin access with no audit trail.",
      "component": "Session management",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.3.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-613"],
      "plainLanguage": "Users can't properly log out, so if they leave their computer, the next person who sits down could use their admin access.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement SAML single logout",
        "given": "a user is authenticated via SAML",
        "when": "they initiate logout",
        "then": "the portal must invalidate the local session, send a logout request to the IdP, and handle IdP-initiated logout requests"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Stale IdP metadata allows compromised certificates",
      "scenario": "The corporate IdP rotates its signing certificate, but the portal continues using cached metadata with the old certificate. An attacker who compromised the old certificate can sign fraudulent SAML assertions that the portal still accepts.",
      "component": "SAML assertion processor",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "Employees sign in to the internal admin portal through SAML single sign-on with the corporate identity provider"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.6"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-295"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the system keeps using old security certificates after they've been replaced, someone who stole the old certificate could still use it to forge logins.",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Regularly refresh IdP metadata and certificates",
        "given": "the portal trusts an IdP certificate",
        "when": "time passes or certificate rotation occurs",
        "then": "the portal must fetch fresh IdP metadata at least daily and support certificate rollover periods"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This threat model identifies 14 concrete threats across all STRIDE categories, focusing on SAML-specific attacks, XML processing vulnerabilities, and authorization bypass risks. Each threat includes verbatim quotes from the context, ASVS requirements, CWE mappings, and plain-language explanations.
